<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cadair Idris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cader Idris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cadair Idris is a mountain in Gwynedd, north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, rising to 893 metres at Penygadair at the southern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> National Park. Its name, 'chair of Idris', refers to a legendary giant or to Idris ap Gwyddno, a 7th-century king of Meirionnydd. The glacial lake Llyn Cau and its cliffs make it one of the best-known mountains in Wales.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cadair-idris.jpg" alt="Cadair Idris" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NotFromUtrecht · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cadair_Idris_wide_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
